Input: {"question": "What is the population of Pilica?", "original_source_segments": {"D1S1": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d00", "start": 0, "end": 138, "text": "Gmina Pilica Gmina Pilica is an urban-rural gmina (administrative district) in Zawiercie County, Silesian Voivodeship, in southern Poland."}, "D1S2": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d00", "start": 139, "end": 262, "text": "Its seat is the town of Pilica, which lies approximately east of Zawiercie and north-east of the regional capital Katowice."}, "D1S3": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d00", "start": 263, "end": 455, "text": "The gmina covers an area of , and as of 2006 its total population is 9,117 (out of which the population of Pilica amounts to 1,971, and the population of the rural part of the gmina is 7,146)."}, "D1S4": {"document_number": 1, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d00", "start": 456, "end": 652, "text": "Apart from the town of Pilica, Gmina Pilica contains the villages and settlements of Biskupice, Cisowa, Dobra, Dobra-Kolonia, Dobraków, Dzwono-Sierbowice, Dzwonowice, Jasieniec, Kidów, Kleszczowa,"}, "D2S1": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 0, "end": 127, "text": "Pilica, Silesian Voivodeship Pilica is a town in Zawiercie County, Silesian Voivodeship, Poland, with 1,948 inhabitants (2004)."}, "D2S2": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 128, "end": 199, "text": "Since the beginning of its existence, Pilica was part of Lesser Poland."}, "D2S3": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 200, "end": 306, "text": "In the years 1115–1118 testament of Bolesław III Krzywousty, Pilica became part of the district of Kraków."}, "D2S4": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 307, "end": 352, "text": "The probable date of its town rights is 1393."}, "D2S5": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 353, "end": 503, "text": "Several years after the uprising of 1 January 1870, Pilica lost its town rights under the Tsar's ukase of June 1, 1869, until it was regained in 1994."}, "D2S6": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 504, "end": 592, "text": "Jews are first mentioned in Pilica in 1581, when they are accused of insulting the host."}, "D2S7": {"document_number": 2, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d01", "start": 593, "end": 596, "text": "The"}, "D3S1": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d02", "start": 0, "end": 163, "text": "Pilica, Masovian Voivodeship Pilica is a village in the administrative district of Gmina Warka, within Grójec County, Masovian Voivodeship, in east-central Poland."}, "D3S2": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d02", "start": 164, "end": 243, "text": "It lies approximately north-east of Warka, east of Grójec, and south of Warsaw."}, "D3S3": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d02", "start": 244, "end": 391, "text": "The village has a population of 210 and lies on the higher, left bank of the Pilica River, about 9 km upstream from where it now joins the Vistula."}, "D3S4": {"document_number": 3, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d02", "start": 392, "end": 602, "text": "In the Middle Ages, the settlement's location on the western scarp of the Vistula River valley marked the then confluence of the two rivers, resulting in the village sharing the name of the river; however there"}, "D4S1": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d03", "start": 0, "end": 140, "text": "Tower in Siedlęcin contains one of the best preserved medieval frescos in Poland , and the world 's only in situ depiction of Sir Lancelot ."}, "D4S2": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d03", "start": 141, "end": 375, "text": "World War II sites include the museum at the former Nazi German Gross-Rosen concentration camp and memorials at the sites of other Nazi camps and prisons , at the sites of Nazi massacres , and to the Polish resistance movement , etc ."}, "D4S3": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d03", "start": 376, "end": 501, "text": "A portion of the underground structures built as part of the unfinished Nazi German Project Riese is available for tourists ."}, "D4S4": {"document_number": 4, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d03", "start": 502, "end": 550, "text": "One of the largest war cemeteries in Poland , of"}, "D5S1": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d04", "start": 0, "end": 118, "text": "201 ] The Sejm is elected under proportional representation according to the d'Hondt method for vote-seat conversion ."}, "D5S2": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d04", "start": 119, "end": 275, "text": "[ 202 ] The Senate is elected under the first-past-the-post electoral system , with one senator being returned from each of the one hundred constituencies ."}, "D5S3": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d04", "start": 276, "end": 430, "text": "[ 203 ] The Senate has the right to amend or reject a statute passed by the Sejm , but the Sejm may override the Senate 's decision with a majority vote ."}, "D5S4": {"document_number": 5, "doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d04", "start": 431, "end": 547, "text": "[ 204 ] The Sejm is the lower house of the parliament of Poland With the exception of ethnic minority parties , only"}}, "original_cards": [{"doc_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d00", "card_id": "ramdocs-0394-9a9e2aa67c0a:d00:c0", "entity": "Pilica", "scope": "population of the town of Pilica", "claim": "As of 2006, the population of Pilica amounts to 1,971.", "answer": "1,971", "quote": "The gmina covers an area of , and as of 2006 its total population is 9,117 (out of which the population of Pilica amounts to